---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/usgaap/asc280.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope & Applicability" },
        { id: "s3", label: "The Chief Operating Decision Maker (CODM)" },
        { id: "s4", label: "The 75% Reporting Sufficiency Test" },
        { id: "s5", label: "ASU 2023-07 – Significant Segment Expense Disclosure" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Worked Example – Applying the 10% Tests" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Entity-Wide Disclosures" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s10", label: "ASC 280 vs IFRS 8" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Related Topics" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Glossary" },
        { id: "history", label: "History & Development" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci"><div class="ctitle ci">The "management approach" – seeing the business through management's own eyes</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">ASC 280 pioneered the "MANAGEMENT APPROACH" to segment reporting: rather than defining segments using externally imposed criteria (e.g. industry classification codes), segments are identified based on the INTERNAL organisational structure management itself actually uses to run the business and allocate resources – maximising relevance by showing users the business the SAME way management sees it.</div></div>
      <div class="card cs"><div class="ctitle cs">The direct model IFRS 8 was built on</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">When the IASB developed IFRS 8, it deliberately modelled it CLOSELY on the pre-existing US standard (SFAS 131, now ASC 280) – making ASC 280 one of the very few Topics in the entire Codification where an IFRS standard was essentially built by ADOPTING an existing US GAAP standard's core methodology, rather than the two frameworks converging independently or from different starting points.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope &amp; Applicability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Public entities specifically</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">ASC 280 applies to PUBLIC entities – those with equity or debt securities traded in a public market, or required to file with a regulatory agency for the purpose of issuing securities. Private companies are NOT required to present segment disclosures, closely mirroring IFRS 8's own scope limitation to entities whose securities are (or are in the process of being) publicly traded.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The Chief Operating Decision Maker (CODM)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">A function, not necessarily a single named individual</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">The CODM is a FUNCTION – identifying the person or GROUP of persons responsible for allocating resources and assessing the performance of the entity's operating segments. This is typically the CEO, but could be a group such as the executive management committee – the identification depends on FACTS AND CIRCUMSTANCES, not a fixed job title, exactly mirroring IFRS 8's own functional (rather than titular) CODM concept.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>The 75% Reporting Sufficiency Test</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Ensuring enough of the business is actually disclosed</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">If the TOTAL external revenue reported by all identified reportable segments is LESS THAN 75% of the entity's total consolidated revenue, ADDITIONAL operating segments must be identified as reportable (even if they individually fail the 10% tests) until at least 75% of total revenue is captured within reportable segments – again, the SAME 75% threshold used under IFRS 8.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>ASU 2023-07 – Significant Segment Expense Disclosure</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A genuinely recent enhancement, ahead of an equivalent IFRS 8 amendment</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">ASU 2023-07 (effective for fiscal years beginning after 15 December 2023) EXPANDED ASC 280's disclosure requirements, requiring disclosure of SIGNIFICANT SEGMENT EXPENSES that are REGULARLY PROVIDED to the CODM and included in the reported segment profit/loss measure, along with a description of "other" segment items not separately disclosed. This is one of the most SUBSTANTIVE recent enhancements to US segment reporting, going beyond what IFRS 8 currently requires in equivalent detail – though the IASB has separately pursued its own primary financial statements project addressing some related disclosure gaps, without yet reaching an identical amendment.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Worked Example – Applying the 10% Tests</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Identifying reportable segments from raw operating segment data</div>
      <div class="worked"><div class="wk-title">Example: Four operating segments, testing against the revenue threshold</div><div class="wk-body">An entity has four operating segments with total revenue (including intersegment) of $50,000,000, $30,000,000, $15,000,000, and $5,000,000 – combined total $100,000,000.</div><div class="wk-answer">10% threshold = $10,000,000. Segments A ($50M), B ($30M), and C ($15M) EXCEED the threshold and are reportable on the revenue test alone. Segment D ($5M) does NOT meet the revenue test, but must still be checked against the profit/loss and assets tests before being excluded – if it fails all three, it may be combined into an "All Other" category rather than separately reported, PROVIDED the 75% sufficiency test (Section 4) is still satisfied by the three reportable segments already identified.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Entity-Wide Disclosures</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Required even for single-segment entities</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">Certain ENTITY-WIDE disclosures are required REGARDLESS of the segment structure – including revenue from external customers for each group of similar products/services, revenue and long-lived assets attributed to the entity's home country versus all foreign countries in aggregate (and by individual foreign country if material), and information about MAJOR CUSTOMERS (a single external customer representing 10% or more of total revenue) – again mirroring IFRS 8's own entity-wide disclosure requirements closely.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Summary reference (see Sections 5–7 for full detail)</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">In addition to the per-segment measures, entities must provide a RECONCILIATION of total reportable segment revenues, profit/loss, and assets to the corresponding consolidated totals – ensuring the segment disclosures tie back cleanly to the primary financial statements, consistent with IFRS 8's own reconciliation requirement.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Identifying the CODM, particularly for committee-based or matrix management structures</span><span>Determining whether operating segments genuinely meet the aggregation criteria</span><span>Identifying which segment expenses are "significant" and "regularly provided" to the CODM under ASU 2023-07</span></div></div>
      <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Allocating shared/corporate costs to segments where the CODM's own internal reporting requires an allocation methodology</span></div></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>ASC 280 vs IFRS 8</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison – ASC 280 vs IFRS 8</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">US GAAP (ASC 280)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IFRS 8)</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Management approach</span><span>Originated here; adopted virtually unchanged by IFRS 8</span><span>Directly modelled on ASC 280 – near-identical core methodology</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>10% quantitative thresholds / 75% sufficiency test</span><span>Yes, as described</span><span>Identical thresholds and mechanics</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Significant segment expense disclosure</span><span>REQUIRED since ASU 2023-07 (effective FY2024)</span><span>No equivalent amendment yet issued – a current, real-time divergence pending further IASB action</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Scope</span><span>Public entities (SEC filers/publicly traded securities)</span><span>Entities whose debt/equity is traded (or in the process of being traded) in a public market – closely parallel</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">ASC 280 and IFRS 8 remain among the most tightly converged pairs in the entire US GAAP/IFRS landscape – the 2023 US-specific expense disclosure enhancement is the first genuinely notable divergence to emerge in years between these two otherwise near-identical standards.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every ASC 280 event lands</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Segment-level disclosures</span><span>Notes only</span><span>No direct impact on any consolidated primary statement</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Reconciliation of segment totals to consolidated figures</span><span>Notes only</span><span>Ties segment disclosures back to the consolidated BS/IS totals</span></div>
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Related Topics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Connection</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 270</span><span>Segment information is also required in interim (quarterly) reports for public entities</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 350</span><span>Goodwill impairment testing uses reporting units that are frequently, though not always, aligned with operating segments</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 810</span><span>Consolidation – supplies the consolidated totals segment disclosures must reconcile to</span></div>
      </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn"><div class="ctitle cn">Quick-reference glossary preview</div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Operating segment</div><div class="glossary-def">A component of an entity meeting all three defining criteria – the base unit of analysis before aggregation and reportability testing.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Reportable segment</div><div class="glossary-def">An operating segment (or aggregation of segments) that meets the quantitative thresholds, or is otherwise designated reportable, and is separately disclosed.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>

    <Glossary entries={[
      { term: "Chief operating decision maker (CODM)", definition: "The function – an individual or group – responsible for allocating resources to, and assessing the performance of, an entity's operating segments." },
      { term: "Management approach", definition: "The segment identification philosophy basing reportable segments on the entity's actual internal management reporting structure, rather than externally imposed classification criteria." }
    ]} />

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.ASC280 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>

